Terminal emulator and terminal view
====================================

The Java sources under com/termux/terminal and com/termux/view are vendored from the
`terminal-emulator` and `terminal-view` libraries of Termux:

    https://github.com/termux/termux-app, commit 8629e632fcb95da272221be327db653fb24befe9

Those two libraries derive from Terminal Emulator for Android by Jack Palevich
(https://github.com/jackpal/Android-Terminal-Emulator) and are released under the Apache License,
Version 2.0, as termux-app's LICENSE.md states for them (the rest of termux-app is GPLv3 and none
of it is vendored here). The licence text is in the LICENSE file beside this one.

Remote Control draws a shell that runs on another machine (protocol amendment A38), so the
libraries were changed where they assumed a local process. Every modified file says so at its top.

Removed
-------
- terminal/JNI.java: it created and waited for a local subprocess through a native library,
  which this app neither ships nor needs.

Modified
--------
- terminal/TerminalSession.java: rewritten as a remote session. No subprocess, pseudo-terminal,
  threads or JNI; bytes from the network are handed in with feed(), the emulator's and the
  person's bytes leave through an Output the owner sets, which also hears every change of the
  grid's size. getPid(), getCwd() and the process bookkeeping are removed.
- view/textselection/TextSelectionCursorController.java: the Copy and Paste items use the
  platform's own words (android.R.string.copy, android.R.string.paste) instead of the library's
  string resources, which are not packaged; the More item, which opened Termux's own context
  menu, is not added.
- view/TerminalRenderer.java: the cells are SwiftTerm's, 1.25 of the type size tall, with each
  row's glyphs at the top of its cell, and the block cursor is a faint outline while the view does
  not have the keyboard, as the iPhone's terminal draws them.
- view/TerminalView.java: rows start at the top of the view to match (getColumnAndRow,
  updateSize), the renderer is told whether the view has focus, and a change of focus redraws.
- view/textselection/TextSelectionHandleView.java: the selection handles are the platform's own
  (android:textSelectHandleLeft and textSelectHandleRight from the theme) instead of the
  library's drawable resources, which are not packaged.

Kept unchanged
--------------
Everything else, including the sixel and iTerm image support (ITermImage, TerminalBitmap,
TerminalSixel), which is plain Java over android.graphics and needs no native code.
